\description{
Main entry point of bayome. The arguments follow \code{maaslin3::maaslin3()}
so that an existing MaAsLin3 call can be reused by changing only the
function name. Bayesian settings are optional extras.
}
\details{
bayome fits a two-part (hurdle) model:
\itemize{
\item \strong{prevalence}: presence/absence, binomial likelihood with logit link,
using all samples;
\item \strong{abundance}: transformed abundance (log2 of the normalised value by
default), Gaussian likelihood, using only the samples where the feature
is present.
}

By default (\code{shrinkage = "joint"}) all features and both parts are fitted
in one INLA model. Prevalence effects are estimated feature by feature;
abundance effects are shrunk towards what each feature's prevalence effect
suggests, \code{u_A = lambda * u_P + e}, with one \code{lambda} per covariate
estimated from all features and \code{e} shared across features.

As in MaAsLin3, the pipeline is normalise, filter, transform, then fit.

\strong{Median comparison.} For each coefficient (for example \code{diagnosisCD})
and posterior draw, the median across features is computed, and each
feature's draw is compared with it: \code{delta = beta - median}. Features are
fitted separately, so their posteriors are independent and the draws can
be combined directly; the posterior of \code{delta} therefore includes the
uncertainty of the median, and the fact that each feature contributes to
it. Rows with an \code{error}, and coefficients present in fewer than three
features, do not take part. Draws are random: call \code{set.seed()} first for
reproducible results.
}
